+3x \\\\ -z-2x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979728/state.json" "file_sha256" "61e0c9674e24eb63ece877fe2e32be869246f2c54dfc72c7c224b3b79bf5f6d6"} {"question" "q-74944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, rewrite the order of the variables in the usual way:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5z \\\\ 3x-y+z \\\\ -2x+3y-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe require a $3\\times 3$ matrix $T$ such that\n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5z \\\\ 3x-y+z \\\\ -2x+3y-z\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5z \\\\ 3x-y+z \\\\ -2x+3y-z\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979728/state.json" "file_sha256" "61e0c9674e24eb63ece877fe2e32be869246f2c54dfc72c7c224b3b79bf5f6d6"} {"question" "q-74944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979728/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f4e7091e4ee56be98695f773fd266de658bdf2c1b27307304b6fc62f088bb32"} {"question" "q-74944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979728/state.json" "file_sha256" "61e0c9674e24eb63ece877fe2e32be869246f2c54dfc72c7c224b3b79bf5f6d6"} {"question" "q-74944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer